"""Practice the Test 1 output structures using only the advance practice CSV."""
def bridge_slides(cohort):
 from test_revision_slides import text,code,practice
 unit={'CML3111':'°C','CML3112':'kW','CML3113':'kWh/t'}[cohort]
 slides = [
 text('Practice columns and test columns', 'Practice reading corresponds to test record_id. Practice machine corresponds to test equipment_id.\n\nPractice load corresponds to test load_pct. Practice ambient corresponds to test ambient_c. target has the same name.\n\nThe calculations are the same. Use the column names in the file you are working with.', 'The practice CSV stays unchanged. Read each pair aloud. A record identifier labels an observation; it is not a timestamp. The test notebook explains its column names before the tasks. The next examples use practice names only.'),
 text('Tables and lists of records', 'A DataFrame is a labelled table. A list of dictionaries stores the same rows as separate Python records.\n\ntable.to_dict("records") returns one dictionary per row. pd.DataFrame(records) turns those records back into a table.\n\nThe test supplies a conversion helper. You may use it when returning cleaned records.', 'Use the retained raw table from the earlier practice cleaning. Each dictionary has reading, machine, load, target and ambient keys. The list preserves row order. These retained rows have no missing values. The supplied test helper also handles missing values when converting the original input.'),
 code('The retained rows as records', 'practice_records = raw.to_dict("records")\nprint(practice_records[0])\nrestored = pd.DataFrame(practice_records)\nprint(len(restored))', 'Expected: first record has reading 1 and target 10. Row count: 4.', ['[0] selects the first record.','The table has the same columns and values.'], 'First, convert the four cleaned rows into a list. The first dictionary describes M1 at load 20 with target 10 and ambient 20. The integer row labels of the DataFrame are not a column in these dictionaries. Rebuilding a DataFrame gives the same four measurements.', 'assert len(practice_records)==4; assert practice_records[0]["target"]==10; assert restored.equals(raw.reset_index(drop=True))'),
 text('Missing values during conversion', 'The test helper uses astype(object) so cells can hold Python None.\n\nnotna() marks known values. where(mask, None) keeps those values and replaces missing cells with None.\n\nThe helper finishes with to_dict("records"). It is supplied so you do not have to invent a missing-value converter.', 'Explain these as steps inside a provided helper. astype(object) changes the storage type, not the measured quantities. Missing numeric CSV cells are normally NaN; the function interface uses None. The next practice helper mirrors this supplied convenience.'),
 code('A helper for the original CSV', 'def as_records(table):\n    objects = table.astype(object)\n    return objects.where(table.notna(), None).to_dict("records")\n\noriginal_records = as_records(source)\nprint(len(original_records))', 'Expected: 7 original records, including the rows to remove.', ['source is the original uploaded CSV.'], 'The helper accepts any table. It uses that argument instead of a global variable. The return statement gives a list back to the caller. original_records still contains the duplicate, the missing target and the invalid load.', 'assert len(original_records)==7; assert original_records[5]["target"] is None'),
 text('A cleaning function follows the same steps', 'def names the function and its input. Every indented line belongs to that function.\n\nBuild a table from the input, apply the earlier cleaning rules, and return records using the helper.\n\nCalling the function with different records must calculate a new result.', 'This combines already practised operations. Students should explain the input, each row removal, the replacement median and the return type. No marks depend on memorising the four practice target values.'),
 code('The complete practice cleaning function', 'def revision_clean(records):\n    table = pd.DataFrame(records)\n    table = table.drop_duplicates("reading")\n    table = table.dropna(subset=["target"])\n    table = table.loc[table["load"].between(0, 100)].copy()\n    middle = table["ambient"].median()\n    table["ambient"] = table["ambient"].fillna(middle)\n    return as_records(table)', 'Returns a new list of cleaned records. It does not print the answer.', ['The input is records, not a global CSV table.'], 'Trace seven input rows to six after removing the repeated reading, five after dropping the missing target, and four after checking load. The retained known ambient values are 20, 30 and 40. Their median is 30. Returning through as_records preserves the column names and row order.', 'assert len(revision_clean(original_records))==4; assert len(original_records)==7'),
 code('Calling the cleaning function', 'cleaned_records = revision_clean(original_records)\nprint(len(original_records))\nprint(len(cleaned_records))\nprint(cleaned_records[1]["ambient"])', 'Expected: 7 original rows, 4 cleaned rows, replacement ambient 30.0.', ['The original list remains available.','[1] selects the second retained record.'], 'The function call returns a new list assigned to cleaned_records. The second retained record originally had missing ambient. The two successive bracket operations first choose a record, then its ambient field.', 'assert len(cleaned_records)==4; assert cleaned_records[1]["ambient"]==30; assert original_records[1]["ambient"] is None'),
 text('Summary values inside a dictionary', 'len(table) counts rows. target.min() and target.max() find the smallest and largest values.\n\nA return dictionary gives each result a name: count, mean, minimum, maximum and group_means.\n\nA grouped Series.to_dict() converts its equipment labels and means into another dictionary.', 'Here the targets are 10, 20, 30, 40. Count is 4, mean 25, minimum 10 and maximum 40. group_means maps M1 to 15 and M2 to 35. Unlike to_dict("records") on a DataFrame, to_dict() on a Series produces one mapping.'),
 code('A summary function', 'def revision_summary(records):\n    table = pd.DataFrame(records)\n    values = table["target"]\n    groups = table.groupby("machine")["target"].mean()\n    return {"count": len(table),\n            "mean": float(values.mean()),\n            "minimum": float(values.min()),\n            "maximum": float(values.max()),\n            "group_means": groups.to_dict()}', 'Returns named numerical values and a dictionary of machine means.', ['Braces enclose key–value pairs.'], 'Each quoted key names one output. Colons connect keys to values. Commas separate entries. float converts a pandas numerical result into a Python decimal number. A multiline dictionary may continue until its closing brace. Keep full numerical precision.', 'assert revision_summary(cleaned_records)=={"count":4,"mean":25.,"minimum":10.,"maximum":40.,"group_means":{"M1":15.,"M2":35.}}'),
 code('Reading the returned summary', 'summary = revision_summary(cleaned_records)\nprint(summary["count"], summary["mean"])\nprint(summary["minimum"], summary["maximum"])\nprint(summary["group_means"])', 'Expected: 4 and 25.0; 10.0 and 40.0; M1: 15.0, M2: 35.0.', ['Use a key to retrieve a named answer.'], 'First call the function with the retained records. Then retrieve results from its returned dictionary. Printed results help you check your work, but the grader receives the returned dictionary itself.', 'assert summary["maximum"]==40; assert summary["group_means"]["M2"]==35'),
 text('A Figure is an object you can return', 'plt.subplots() creates a Figure and its plotting Axes. fig holds the whole chart; ax holds the drawing area.\n\nax.scatter, ax.bar and ax.hist draw on those Axes. set_xlabel, set_ylabel and set_title add labels.\n\nreturn fig gives the chart to the caller. plt.show() only displays it.', 'Displaying a chart is different from returning a chart. The test asks for actual Figure objects in a list, in scatter, bar, histogram order. Students should not return filenames, screenshots or dictionaries describing charts.'),
 code('A function returning a scatter Figure', 'def revision_scatter(table):\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n    ax.scatter(table["load"], table["target"])\n    ax.set_xlabel("Load (%)")\n    ax.set_ylabel("Target ('+unit+')")\n    ax.set_title("Practice load and target")\n    return fig', 'The returned Figure contains four points from the practice CSV.', ['fig and ax receive the two created objects.'], 'The input table contains cleaned rows. The scatter combines load and target from each row. All chart labels belong to ax. The return statement returns fig, not ax. This function does not modify the input.', 'assert callable(revision_scatter)'),
 text('Alphabetical order for group means', 'groupby("machine")["target"].mean() calculates one mean per machine.\n\nsort_index() orders that Series by its labels. M1 comes before M2.\n\nUse the same ordered Series for both the bar labels and heights.', 'The index of the grouped Series is its machine labels. Sorting by index sorts those labels, not the mean values. Sorting heights independently would attach the wrong value to a machine.'),
 code('A function returning ordered bars', 'def revision_bars(table):\n    means = table.groupby("machine")["target"].mean()\n    means = means.sort_index()\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n    ax.bar(means.index, means.values)\n    ax.set_xlabel("Machine")\n    ax.set_ylabel("Mean target ('+unit+')")\n    ax.set_title("Practice machine means")\n    return fig', 'Expected bar order and height: M1 = 15, then M2 = 35.', ['sort_index sorts the group labels.'], 'Compute the means first, then put their labels in alphabetical order. Pass labels and corresponding means to bar. Return the Figure object. With more groups, the same function still calculates all group means.', 'assert callable(revision_bars)'),
 text('Ten histogram bins', 'The earlier two-bin example explained counting. The test asks for ten equal-width bins.\n\nax.hist(values, bins=10) divides the range from the minimum to the maximum into ten equal intervals.\n\nFor practice targets 10 to 40, each bin is 3 units wide. Empty bins still count as bins.', 'Ten bins means ten intervals, not ten readings. The first bin starts at 10 and the last ends at 40. The rightmost edge is included. There are only four readings, so many bins are empty. Do not reuse the earlier fixed boundaries [0, 25, 50] for the test.'),
 code('A function returning a ten-bin histogram', 'def revision_histogram(table):\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n    ax.hist(table["target"], bins=10)\n    ax.set_xlabel("Target ('+unit+')")\n    ax.set_ylabel("Number of observations")\n    ax.set_title("Practice target distribution")\n    return fig', 'Expected: 10 bars with total count 4, spanning targets 10 to 40.', ['bins=10 lets matplotlib calculate the edges.'], 'A histogram Figure contains rectangular bars, including bars with zero height. The heights sum to the number of records. The unit belongs on the target axis; the other axis counts observations.', 'assert callable(revision_histogram)'),
 code('Returning the three Figures in order', 'def revision_charts(records):\n    table = pd.DataFrame(records)\n    return [revision_scatter(table),\n            revision_bars(table),\n            revision_histogram(table)]\n\nfigures = revision_charts(cleaned_records)\nplt.show()', 'The list holds scatter, bar and histogram Figures in that order.', ['Each helper returns one real Figure.'], 'The square brackets form a list of the three returned objects. A helper must be defined before this function is called. The actual test requires a build_charts function; helpers are allowed. The chart list is the returned answer, while show displays it for inspection.', 'assert len(figures)==3; assert len(figures[0].axes[0].collections[0].get_offsets())==4; assert len(figures[2].axes[0].patches)==10; assert sum(p.get_height() for p in figures[2].axes[0].patches)==4'),
 text('The gap between group means', 'The group gap is the largest group mean minus the smallest group mean.\n\nOur machine means are 15 and 35. The gap is 35 − 15 = 20.\n\nThis differs from the individual target range: 40 − 10 = 30.', 'Emphasise the difference between a group mean and an individual record. Use group means for group_gap. Its unit is the target unit. The observed gap does not establish why the equipment differs.'),
 code('Calculating the group gap', 'table = pd.DataFrame(cleaned_records)\nmeans = table.groupby("machine")["target"].mean()\ngap = float(means.max() - means.min())\nprint(gap)', 'Expected: group gap 20.0 '+unit+'.', ['max and min operate on group means here.'], 'The means Series contains 15 and 35. Taking their maximum and minimum produces a gap of 20. This is an observed difference in these synthetic data, not a recommendation to replace equipment.', 'assert gap==20'),
 text('A finding contains a value and a unit', 'A finding is a small dictionary, for example {"value": 25.0, "unit": "'+unit+'"}.\n\nThe outer dictionary names the finding. The inner dictionary stores its number and unit.\n\nUse observations for count. Use the target unit for mean, minimum, maximum and group_gap.', 'A nested dictionary is a dictionary containing another dictionary. Keys value and unit are literal names required by the test. Correct unit spellings such as °C, deg C and degrees C are equivalent. A quantity measured in kWh/t cannot be labelled kW.'),
 code('A helper for a value with its unit', 'def with_unit(value, unit):\n    return {"value": value, "unit": unit}\n\nexample = with_unit(25.0, "'+unit+'")\nprint(example["value"], example["unit"])', 'Expected: 25.0 '+unit+'.', ['The function returns one inner dictionary.'], 'The first argument is the number. The second is its unit string. The helper constructs the inner dictionary. The next function uses it to give every finding an explicit unit.', 'assert example["value"]==25'),
 code('Returning the five practice findings', 'def revision_findings(records):\n    result = revision_summary(records)\n    means = pd.Series(result["group_means"])\n    gap = float(means.max() - means.min())\n    unit = "'+unit+'"\n    return {"count": with_unit(result["count"], "observations"),\n            "mean": with_unit(result["mean"], unit),\n            "minimum": with_unit(result["minimum"], unit),\n            "maximum": with_unit(result["maximum"], unit),\n            "group_gap": with_unit(gap, unit)}', 'Returns five findings, each containing value and unit.', ['The count has a different unit.'], 'Reuse the summary calculation to avoid inconsistent values. pd.Series turns the group_means dictionary into a Series whose index is the machine labels. Calculate the gap on that Series. The returned outer dictionary has five exact keys. Each entry is an inner dictionary returned by with_unit.', 'assert revision_findings(cleaned_records)["group_gap"]["value"]==20'),
 code('Checking the returned findings', 'findings = revision_findings(cleaned_records)\nprint(findings["count"])\nprint(findings["mean"])\nprint(findings["group_gap"])', 'Expected values: count 4, mean 25.0, group gap 20.0.', ['Two keys reach a nested value.'], 'count returns {value: 4, unit: observations}. mean returns {value: 25.0, unit: the cohort target unit}. group_gap returns value 20 with that same target unit. To get just the gap number, use findings["group_gap"]["value"].', 'assert findings["count"]=={"value":4,"unit":"observations"}; assert findings["mean"]["value"]==25'),
 *practice('Practice: another input to your functions', 'Use original_records[:2] to select the first two original records. Clean this smaller input.\n\nCall your summary, charts and findings functions on that result.\n\nPredict the retained count, mean and group gap before running.', 'The two input records have unique IDs, known targets and valid loads. Both remain.\n\nMean target: 15. Only M1 remains, so group gap is 0. The missing ambient value becomes 20.\n\nThe functions calculate from the supplied argument, even when its length changes.', '[:2] is a slice taking positions 0 and 1. Demonstrate calls revision_clean(original_records[:2]), then pass that result to each later function. There is no assessment submission for this practice.'),
 text('What a filled value can and cannot tell us', 'A median replacement is an estimate, not a recovered measurement. It can change summaries and patterns.\n\nKeep the original data and state the replacement rule.\n\nA pattern in synthetic practice data does not establish real equipment performance.', 'Ask which ambient reading was actually missing: reading 2. Filling it with 30 does not prove that the true temperature was 30. Keeping source lets us distinguish recorded and estimated values.'),
 text('An identifier is not a clock', 'reading and record_id identify observations. Their numbers do not tell us elapsed time.\n\nA time trend needs an actual timestamp or a documented time sequence.\n\nAn equipment mean describes the observed sample. Investigate representative measured data before making operational decisions.', 'Reading 4 is not evidence of hour 4. An ID can be assigned during collection or import without representing time. Likewise, M2 having a higher sample mean does not prove it is faulty. Do not infer causes or safety decisions from this teaching dataset.'),
 ]

 for s in slides:
  if s.get("kind")=="code" and len(s["code"].splitlines())>=9:s["code_size"]=25
 return slides
